    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Commuting in Waterloo Region\"\ndescription: \"One sentence for the listing card: the question and the answer.\"\ndate: \"2026-09-16\"\ncategories: [Snapshot]   # first entry is the post type: Snapshot (under 5 min) or Deep dive; then 2 to 3 topics\nimage: images/thumbnail.png\ndraft: true\n---\n\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\n<!-- Opening paragraph: the question this post asks and the one-line answer. -->\n\n## First finding as a plain-language heading\n\n<!-- One or two paragraphs, then one chart. -->\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-commuting-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"429\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-commuting.png\" alt=\"One horizontal bar per municipality, each split into three parts: the share of its workers who work in their own municipality, the share who work elsewhere in Waterloo Region, and the share who work outside it. Cambridge: Elsewhere in the Region 16 per cent, In their own municipality 56 per cent, Outside the Region 28 per cent. Kitchener: Elsewhere in the Region 37 per cent, In their own municipality 47 per cent, Outside the Region 16 per cent. North Dumfries: Elsewhere in the Region 54 per cent, In their own municipality 21 per cent, Outside the Region 24 per cent. Waterloo: Elsewhere in the Region 43 per cent, In their own municipality 45 per cent, Outside the Region 12 per cent. Wellesley: Elsewhere in the Region 51 per cent, In their own municipality 32 per cent, Outside the Region 17 per cent. Wilmot: Elsewhere in the Region 54 per cent, In their own municipality 31 per cent, Outside the Region 15 per cent. Woolwich: Elsewhere in the Region 43 per cent, In their own municipality 39 per cent, Outside the Region 18 per cent.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"423\">\n</picture>\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-commuting-mode-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"968\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-commuting-mode.png\" alt=\"Three panels of blue horizontal bars, each on its own scale, one row per municipality, showing the share of commuters who travel to work by car, truck or van, by public transit, and by active transportation (walking or cycling). Car, truck or van: North Dumfries 93.5%, Wilmot 93.1%, Cambridge 92.0%, Woolwich 90.3%, Kitchener 87.8%, Waterloo 83.3%, Wellesley 81.7%. Public transit: North Dumfries 0.4%, Wilmot 0.6%, Cambridge 3.4%, Woolwich 0.4%, Kitchener 6.0%, Waterloo 6.5%, Wellesley 0.0%. Active transportation: North Dumfries 4.4%, Wilmot 4.7%, Cambridge 2.9%, Woolwich 7.2%, Kitchener 4.2%, Waterloo 7.4%, Wellesley 13.8%.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"436\">\n</picture>\n\n::: panel-tabset\n### Kitchener\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-flows-kitchener-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"576\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-flows-kitchener.png\" alt=\"Map of Waterloo Region's seven municipalities, each shaded darker the larger the share of Kitchener's workers who work there. 47.2% of Kitchener's workers work in Kitchener. Elsewhere in the Region: Waterloo 15.8%, Cambridge 13.9%, Woolwich 4.3%, North Dumfries 1.3%, Wilmot 1.3%, Wellesley 0.3%. A red arrow points to the most common workplace outside the Region, Guelph, with 6.7%.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"720\">\n</picture>\n\n### Waterloo\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-flows-waterloo-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"576\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-flows-waterloo.png\" alt=\"Map of Waterloo Region's seven municipalities, each shaded darker the larger the share of Waterloo's workers who work there. 45.3% of Waterloo's workers work in Waterloo. Elsewhere in the Region: Kitchener 27.4%, Cambridge 8.2%, Woolwich 5.8%, Wilmot 1.1%, North Dumfries 0.6%, Wellesley 0.5%. A red arrow points to the most common workplace outside the Region, Guelph, with 3.8%.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"720\">\n</picture>\n\n### Cambridge\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-flows-cambridge-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"576\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-flows-cambridge.png\" alt=\"Map of Waterloo Region's seven municipalities, each shaded darker the larger the share of Cambridge's workers who work there. 56.3% of Cambridge's workers work in Cambridge. Elsewhere in the Region: Kitchener 9.9%, Waterloo 3.2%, North Dumfries 1.6%, Woolwich 1.2%, Wilmot 0.4%, Wellesley 0.0%. A red arrow points to the most common workplace outside the Region, Guelph, with 7.8%.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"720\">\n</picture>\n\n### Woolwich\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-flows-woolwich-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"576\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-flows-woolwich.png\" alt=\"Map of Waterloo Region's seven municipalities, each shaded darker the larger the share of Woolwich's workers who work there. 39.6% of Woolwich's workers work in Woolwich. Elsewhere in the Region: Kitchener 16.7%, Waterloo 12.7%, Cambridge 8.6%, Wellesley 4.4%, Wilmot 0.5%, North Dumfries 0.4%. A red arrow points to the most common workplace outside the Region, Guelph, with 7.1%.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"720\">\n</picture>\n\n### Wilmot\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-flows-wilmot-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"576\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-flows-wilmot.png\" alt=\"Map of Waterloo Region's seven municipalities, each shaded darker the larger the share of Wilmot's workers who work there. 31.9% of Wilmot's workers work in Wilmot. Elsewhere in the Region: Kitchener 27.5%, Waterloo 13.8%, Cambridge 9.3%, Woolwich 1.9%, Wellesley 1.4%, North Dumfries 1.0%. A red arrow points to the most common workplace outside the Region, Stratford, with 3.5%.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"720\">\n</picture>\n\n### North Dumfries\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-flows-north-dumfries-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"576\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-flows-north-dumfries.png\" alt=\"Map of Waterloo Region's seven municipalities, each shaded darker the larger the share of North Dumfries's workers who work there. 22.0% of North Dumfries's workers work in North Dumfries. Elsewhere in the Region: Cambridge 32.8%, Kitchener 14.7%, Waterloo 5.1%, Woolwich 1.5%, Wilmot 1.0%, Wellesley 0.3%. A red arrow points to the most common workplace outside the Region, Brant, with 4.3%.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"720\">\n</picture>\n\n### Wellesley\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-flows-wellesley-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"576\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-flows-wellesley.png\" alt=\"Map of Waterloo Region's seven municipalities, each shaded darker the larger the share of Wellesley's workers who work there. 33.0% of Wellesley's workers work in Wellesley. Elsewhere in the Region: Waterloo 16.0%, Woolwich 13.5%, Kitchener 11.8%, Cambridge 5.1%, Wilmot 4.9%, North Dumfries 1.2%. A red arrow points to the most common workplace outside the Region, Guelph, with 3.0%.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"720\">\n</picture>\n\n:::\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-destinations-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"420\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-destinations.png\" alt=\"Horizontal bars ranking the seven municipalities by their share of the Region's commuters who travel to work in another municipality in the Region. Kitchener 28.1%. Waterloo 27.2%. Cambridge 26.5%. Woolwich 10.4%. North Dumfries 3.4%. Wilmot 2.9%. Wellesley 1.5%.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"411\">\n</picture>\n\n::: panel-tabset\n### Where they work\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-inbound-districts-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"406\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-inbound-districts.png\" alt=\"Horizontal bars ranking the seven municipalities by their share of the people who commute into Waterloo Region from outside it. Cambridge 42.2%. Kitchener 24.5%. Waterloo 12.5%. Woolwich 10.6%. North Dumfries 4.0%. Wilmot 3.5%. Wellesley 2.8%.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"411\">\n</picture>\n\n### Where they come from\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-inbound-feeders-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"517\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-inbound-feeders.png\" alt=\"A lollipop chart of the ten municipalities that send the most commuters into Waterloo Region, ranked by each one's share of everyone who commutes in, against an axis along the top in percent. Guelph 16.8%. Brantford/Brant 13.7%. Hamilton 7.7%. Woodstock 5.2%. Centre Wellington 4.1%. Blandford-Blenheim 3.9%. Stratford 3.7%. Mapleton 3.5%. Perth East 3.3%. Mississauga 2.9%.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"541\">\n</picture>\n:::\n\n<picture>\n<source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"figures/fig-first-phone.png\" width=\"403\" height=\"432\">\n<img src=\"figures/fig-first.png\" alt=\"What the chart shows, for screen readers.\" class=\"img-fluid\" width=\"797\" height=\"480\">\n</picture>\n\n## Second finding\n\n\n::: {#tbl-first .cell tbl-cap='Caption shown under the table.'}\n\n:::\n\n\n## Data sources\n\n<!-- Where the data came from, what was excluded and why, and any caveat a careful\n     reader should know: write that here, above the table. The table itself is not\n     written here - the chunk below prints the \"Data sources\" table straight out of\n     this post's README.md, so there is only ever one copy of it.\n\n     Keep this heading as it is. _includes/like.html puts the \"Like this post\"\n     button immediately above it, on the reasoning that the analysis ends here and\n     everything below is reference material. It finds the spot by the id Pandoc\n     builds from the heading text, so rewording the heading does not break the\n     button - it quietly drops it to the foot of the page instead. -->\n\n| File in `data-raw/` | What it is | Source (link) | Licence | Sample | Accessed |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `table_98100462.csv` | Table 98-10-0462-01, commuting destination by mode, age and gender, 2021 census | [Statistics Canada](https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/t1/tbl1/en/tv.action?pid=9810046201) | [Statistics Canada Open Licence](https://www.statcan.gc.ca/en/reference/licence) | Census long form, 25% sample of households | 2026-09-15 |\n| `table_98100459_region.csv`, `table_98100459_members.csv` | Table 98-10-0459-01, commuting flow from place of residence to place of work, 2021 census, cut to commutes with one end in the Region; the members file is the table's own list of places and codes | [Statistics Canada](https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/t1/tbl1/en/tv.action?pid=9810045901) | [Statistics Canada Open Licence](https://www.statcan.gc.ca/en/reference/licence) | Census long form, 25% sample of households | 2026-09-17 |\n| `csd_boundaries.gpkg` | 2021 census subdivision cartographic boundary file (`lcsd000b21a_e`), cut to the Region and the places its residents commute to | [Statistics Canada](https://www12.statcan.gc.ca/census-recensement/2021/geo/sip-pis/boundary-limites/index2021-eng.cfm?year=21) | [Statistics Canada Open Licence](https://www.statcan.gc.ca/en/reference/licence) | None (boundaries) | 2026-09-17 |\n\n## Other sources\n\n<!-- Anything cited that is not data: reports, articles, books, a method borrowed\n     from someone else. Quoted material stays under its owner's terms, never this\n     site's CC BY - see WORKFLOW.md section 9. Delete this section if unused. -->\n\n::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n## Reproducibility and data download\n\n**Reproduce the post**: get the code and data for this post from [`posts/commuting/`](https://github.com/gpberber/chartingwaterlooregion/tree/master/posts/commuting) on GitHub.\n\n**Download the data:** [commuting-data-v1.zip](https://github.com/gpberber/chartingwaterlooregion/releases/download/data-commuting-v1/commuting-data-v1.zip) holds every table used in this post as CSV and Parquet files, an Excel workbook, a data dictionary, and a README with sources and licences. The same dictionary is in the post's [README](https://github.com/gpberber/chartingwaterlooregion/tree/master/posts/commuting#data-dictionary).\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\nR version: R version 4.5.3 (2026-03-11 ucrt)\nOS:        Windows 11 x64 (build 26200)\nRendered:  2026-09-18\nQuarto:    1.9.38\n\n    package version       date         source\n      arrow  25.0.1 2026-08-23 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n conflicted   1.2.0 2023-02-01 CRAN (R 4.2.3)\n      dplyr   1.2.1 2026-04-03 CRAN (R 4.5.2)\n    forcats   1.0.1 2025-09-25 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n    ggplot2   4.0.3 2026-04-22 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n     ggtext   0.2.0 2026-08-28 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n         gt   1.3.0 2026-01-22 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n   gtExtras   0.6.2 2026-01-17 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n       here   1.0.2 2025-09-15 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n    janitor   2.2.1 2024-12-22 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n  lubridate   1.9.5 2026-02-04 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n  patchwork   1.3.2 2025-08-25 CRAN (R 4.4.3)\n      purrr   1.2.2 2026-04-10 CRAN (R 4.5.2)\n      readr   2.2.0 2026-02-19 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n     scales   1.4.0 2025-04-24 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n         sf   1.1-2 2026-07-23 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n    stringr   1.6.0 2025-11-04 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n     tibble   3.3.1 2026-01-11 CRAN (R 4.5.2)\n      tidyr   1.3.2 2025-12-19 CRAN (R 4.5.3)\n  tidyverse   2.0.0 2023-02-22 CRAN (R 4.5.2)\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n:::\n",
